  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nA splitting trough to our west and a ridge to the east is keeping \nmoist southerly to southwesterly flow aloft over much of western \nColorado. Over eastern Utah, a push of drier air is working in from \nthe west as the trough continues to split into a northern stream \ntrough and southern stream closed low. Along the edge of the \nboundary between the much moisture airmass and the drier one to our \nwest, a few orographically driven showers and thunderstorms have \ndeveloped and will continue through this afternoon and evening. The \nmain concerns with these storms will be gusty outflow winds in \nexcess of 50 mph and frequent lightning, along with heavy rain. The \nbest chances for heavy rain and flash flooding will be with storms \nclosest to the moisture boundary. Meanwhile, across southwest \nColorado, a Flood Watch is in effect for the southwest valleys and \nsouthern San Juans. This area has already seen half an inch to an \ninch of precipitation in the last 24 hours, and stands to see \nanother half an inch to an inch by tomorrow evening. As a result, \nthere's concerns for flash flooding in steeper, small basins, as \nwell as potential for rises in mainstem rivers in this region. \n\nThe best of the moisture plume gets shunted to the east by Friday. \nHowever enough above normal moisture will stick around to interact \nwith a passing shortwave Friday afternoon to bring one last round of \nshowers, storms, and heavy rain to the southern and central \nmountains. Through the end of the work week, temperatures remain \nnear to a few degrees below normal, as we remain moist and cloudy. \nTransient ridging and drier westerly to southwesterly flow moves in \nfor the weekend, with temperatures jumping to near or a few degrees \nabove normal. \n\nA return of deep tropical to subtropical moisture is expected early \nnext as troughing once again slides into the West Coast and ridging \ndominates over the middle of the country. Something to watch with \nthis pattern shift is the interaction between the West Coast trough \nand Hurricane Polo in the tropical eastern Pacific. Should the \nremnant moisture from this hurricane get swept up in the \nsouthwesterly flow aloft, the Four Corners region could see \npotentially intense rainfall. The southwest Colorado valleys and the \nsouthern San Juans could be particularly sensitive, thanks to this \nweek's multiple rounds of heavy precipitation. There's a lot that \ncan change about the forecast between now and early next week, but \nthis is a situation to keep an eye on over the coming days. \nRegardless of whether the moisture is tapped from Hurricane Polo, or \njust from more typical subtropical Pacific sources, the return of \nshowers and cloud cover will see temperatures drop below normal once \nmore.",
